{"page": 1, "text": "Visible text of EX-99.1_2026-11-04_a4qfy26earningrelease.htm, sha256 fcebbd07671b195d579c1a4780a29ef729a2537c5fe7c7c13ad768bedab3fe50.\nEX-99.1 2 a4qfy26earningrelease.htm EX-99.1\nFor further information Contact: Trish Scorpio 651-355-4593 Patricia.Scorpio@chsinc.com\nCHS Reports Fiscal Year 2026 Net Income of $1.1 Billion Energy, Grains and Agronomy Performance Driven by Evolving Market Dynamics; Company Intends to Return $300 Million Cash to Owners in 2027\nST. PAUL, Minn. (November 4, 2026) \u2014 CHS Inc., a global agribusiness and the nation's leading cooperative, today reported net income of $1.1 billion for the fiscal year ended August 31, 2026, compared to $597.9 million for fiscal year 2025.\nKey highlights for fiscal year 2026 financial results include: \u2022Consolidated revenues of $39.2 billion for fiscal year 2026 compared to $35.5 billion for fiscal year 2025 with the increase due largely to higher commodity prices. \u2022Our Energy segment recorded strong refining margins, driven by global market dynamics and high refinery production volumes, which were partially offset by record-high expenses for renewable energy credits (RINs). \u2022Our Grains segment benefited from strong oilseed crush and ethanol margins resulting from renewable energy policy impacts, which were partially offset by global trade headwinds and reduced retail margins. \u2022Continued strong performance by our CF Nitrogen investment was offset by lower agronomy volumes, driven by reduced demand related to ongoing weakness in the U.S. farm economy.\n\"Our results demonstrate the strength of our diversified businesses and strategic supply chain investments,\" said Jay Debertin, president and CEO. \"While market and policy conditions continued to shift and create volatility throughout the year, our teams executed exceptionally well across our operations, allowing us to capture opportunities in energy and agriculture markets while continuing to serve our owners.\"\nCHS also reported intended distribution of $300 million in cash patronage and equity redemptions to its owners in fiscal year 2027, an approximately 150% increase over the fiscal year 2026 distribution. The total will be distributed as $150 million in cash patronage and up to $150 million in equity redemptions. When the planned distributions are complete in 2027, CHS will have returned more than $2.7 billion to owners over the past five years.\n\"These planned cash returns to our owners demonstrate the benefits of CHS ownership and the CHS Board of Directors' commitment to sharing profits with our owners and strengthening rural communities, while investing in our businesses,\" Debertin added.\n"}
{"page": 2, "text": "CHS recently announced several significant investments, including a proposed nearly $450 million phosphate production facility to be built in Louisiana in a joint venture with OCP North America and a new approximately $700 million soybean processing facility in Evansville, Wisconsin, which would increase CHS soy processing capacity by two-thirds. Pending project-related approvals, both facilities are expected to be operational in late 2028 or 2029.\nFiscal Year 2026 Business Segment Results\nEnergy This segment includes our refined fuels, propane and lubricants product lines. Pretax earnings of $554.6 million represent a $573.6 million increase versus the prior year and reflect: \u2022Significantly improved refining margins driven by global market dynamics, partially offset by unrealized hedging losses. \u2022Record-high renewable fuels compliance costs, partially offset by reduced blending obligations at our Laurel, Montana, refinery. \u2022Strong operational performance by our refineries enabled by recent capital investments.\nGrains The Grains segment primarily includes our feed grains, oilseeds, wheat, specialty grains and animal nutrition product lines. Pretax earnings of $127.4 million represent an $83.4 million increase versus the prior year and reflect: \u2022Strong oilseed crush margins in the second half of the fiscal year, driven by growing demand due to increasing biofuels blending obligations. \u2022Solid feed grains export volumes, along with strong ethanol margins in response to U.S. policy impacts. \u2022These results were partially offset by continued global trade volatility and weakening retail margins.\nAgronomy This segment includes crop nutrients, crop protection and CF Nitrogen. Pretax earnings of $354.6 million represent a $20.6 million decrease versus the prior year and reflect: \u2022Strong performance by our CF Nitrogen joint venture investment due to favorable market conditions for urea and UAN. \u2022Decreased volumes due to deferred purchasing decisions by U.S. farmers and lower margins in agronomy products as a result of strong competition and negative market dynamics.\nCorporate and Services This segment includes CHS Capital and CHS Hedging, as well as our Ardent Mills and Ventura Foods joint ventures. Pretax earnings of $92.1 million represent a $122.4 million decrease versus the prior year and reflect: \u2022Lower equity method earnings from our joint ventures and gain on sale of a business by Ventura Foods in 2025, which did not reoccur in 2026.\nCHS Inc. Earnings*\nby Segment\n(in thousands $)\nYears Ended August 31,\n2026 2025\nEnergy $ 554,590 $ (19,015)\nGrains 127,378 44,003\nAgronomy 354,640 375,283\nCorporate and Services 92,111 214,501\nIncome before income taxes 1,128,719 614,772\nIncome tax expense 32,737 16,777\nNet income 1,095,982 597,995\nNet income attributable to noncontrolling interests 79 78\nNet income attributable to CHS Inc. $ 1,095,903 $ 597,917\n"}
{"page": 3, "text": "*Earnings is defined as income (loss) before income taxes. Starting in fiscal year 2026, the company's financial segments have changed to align with its new end-to-end product line operating model. As a result of this change, all prior period segment information has been recast to conform to current year presentation.\nCHS Inc. (www.chsinc.com) creates connections to empower agriculture. As a leading global agribusiness and the largest farmer-owned cooperative in the United States, CHS serves customers in 65 countries. We provide critical crop inputs, market access and risk management services that help farmers feed the world. Our diversified agronomy, grains, foods and energy businesses recorded revenues of approximately $39 billion in fiscal year 2026. CHS is committed to reducing our impact on the planet, finding and developing new solutions in agriculture and energy, and investing in ways to build a better future for our owners, customers, employees and communities.\n"}
